## 3. Technology Choices & Rationale

### 3.1 Backend Framework: FastAPI

| Option | Why FastAPI Wins |
|--------|-----------------|
| Flask | No async support, no auto-docs, slower |
| Django | Too heavy for an API-only backend |
| **FastAPI** ✅ | Async-native, auto-generates API docs, built-in validation with Pydantic, SSE streaming support, modern Python |

### 3.2 RAG Framework: LangChain

| Option | Why LangChain Wins |
|--------|-------------------|
| LlamaIndex | Better for pure document Q&A, but less flexible for custom pipelines |
| **LangChain** ✅ | More flexible, larger ecosystem, better for combining RAG + memory + streaming, more community support, easier to customize |

> **Why not LlamaIndex?** LlamaIndex is excellent for "plug and play" document Q&A, but LangChain gives us more control over each step. Since we want custom memory, streaming, and source citations, LangChain's modular approach fits better.

### 3.3 Vector Database: FAISS

| Option | Why FAISS |
|--------|-----------|
| Pinecone | Cloud-hosted, costs money at scale |
| ChromaDB | Good, but FAISS is faster and battle-tested |
| **FAISS** ✅ | Free, fast, runs locally, created by Meta AI, handles millions of vectors, no server needed |

### 3.4 Embedding Model: `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` (via Sentence-Transformers)

| Model | Dims | Speed | Quality | Free? |
|-------|------|-------|---------|-------|
| OpenAI ada-002 | 1536 | Fast | Best | ❌ Paid |
| **all-MiniLM-L6-v2** ✅ | 384 | Very Fast | Great | ✅ Free |
| all-mpnet-base-v2 | 768 | Slower | Better | ✅ Free |

> **Why this one?** Best balance of speed and quality. Runs locally, no API key needed. 384 dimensions keep FAISS storage small. Perfect for educational content.

### 3.5 LLM: Groq (Llama 3.1 8B or Mixtral 8x7B)

| Option | Why Groq + Llama |
|--------|-----------------|
| OpenAI GPT-4 | Expensive, closed-source |
| Ollama (local) | Requires powerful GPU |
| Hugging Face API | Slow, rate-limited |
| **Groq Cloud** ✅ | Free tier (generous limits), blazing fast inference, supports Llama 3.1 8B & Mixtral, streaming support, simple API |

> **Why Groq?** It provides free, ultra-fast inference for open-source models. Llama 3.1 8B is instruction-tuned and excellent for educational Q&A. No GPU needed on your machine. Groq's free tier allows ~30 requests/minute — perfect for development and small-scale deployment.
>
> **Alternative:** If Groq's free tier is insufficient later, we can swap to Google's Gemini API (also has a generous free tier) with a single config change — that's the beauty of our modular architecture.

### 3.6 Frontend: React (Vite)

| Option | Why React + Vite |
|--------|-----------------|
| Next.js | Overkill for a chat widget, SSR not needed |
| Plain HTML/JS | Hard to manage state, no component reuse |
| **React + Vite** ✅ | Fast dev server, component-based, easy state management, simple to embed as a widget |

### 3.7 Database: SQLite → PostgreSQL

| Phase | Database | Why |
|-------|----------|-----|
| Development | **SQLite** | Zero setup, single file, comes with Python |
| Production | **PostgreSQL** | Multi-user, concurrent writes, cloud-hosted options |

> **How the swap works:** We'll use SQLAlchemy (an ORM) to abstract the database. Switching from SQLite to PostgreSQL means changing ONE line in the config: the database URL. No code changes needed.

